\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso down on U.S. taper concerns; real gains\b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2013\par 20:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico and Chile weakened on Tuesday on renewed concerns about a possible withdrawal of U.S. stimulus in coming months, but the real gained as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reassured investors that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is here to stay. The Mexican peso lost 0.4 percent while the Chilean peso slid 0.5 percent as investors paid close attention to comments from U.S. Federal Reserve officials, searching for clues on the timing of an expected slowdown of the bank's bond-buying program. New York Fed President \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  said the bank "certainly wouldn't want to rule out" a reduction in stimulus later this year, something which could trim demand for emerging market assets. In Brazil, however, comments by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  lent support to the real , which gained 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2  Tombini said the program of daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will provide investors with about $60 billion worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and dollar repurchase agreements by year-end, has been working well and will not be changed. The real has gained about 10 percent since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unveiled its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program on Aug 22. The rally intensified last week after the Fed's shock decision not to cut back on stimulus for now, causing Finance Minister Guido Mantega to suggest \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  could slow down the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .       \par     Latin America \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices at 1825 GMT:\par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                        daily %    YTD %\par                                    change   change\par                                            \par                           Latest           \par  Brazil real              2.1965     0.18    -7.12\par                                            \par  Mexico peso             12.8615    -0.40     0.02\par                                            \par  Chile peso             497.7000    -0.54    -3.82\par                                            \par  Colombia peso          1890.010     0.06    -6.56\par                                0           \par  Peru sol                 2.7590    -0.43    -7.54\par                                            \par  Argentina peso           5.7700     0.04   -14.86\par  (interbank)                               \par                                            \par  Argentina peso           9.4200     0.00   -28.03\par  (parallel)                                \par                                            \par  \par     \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Walter Brandimarte and Marilia Carrera; Editing by Krista Hughes)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | M | RBN | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130927e99o00105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
