\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Slightly Up on China Data, German Election\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris   \par \uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2013\par 14:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--Brazil's real strengthened slightly against the dollar in early trading Monday, taking momentum from upbeat industrial data from China and expectations of policy continuity in Europe following the re-election of German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 As of 08:30 a.m. ET, the real traded at BRL2.2090 to the dollar after ending at BRL2.2160 to the dollar Friday, according to Tullett Prebon via Factset.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the real gained some strength early in the session after China's September \uc2 HSBC\uc2  preliminary manufacturing PMI rose to 51.2 from 50.1 in August. That signs of improvement brought encouragement that firming activity in China could benefit Brazilian exports.\par \par \uc2 Improved PMI data in Europe and the re-election of Ms. Merkel to a third term were also bringing hopes that a recovery in Brazil could continue to get support from improving conditions across the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Locally, analysts remained focused on Brazil's fragile balance of payments situation ahead of the release of current account data this week. The country is seen posting a 12-month current account gap of around 3.5% of gross domestic product for August.\par \par \uc2 "The result could remain weak for some months, while statistics don't fully reflect the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation on some items that are more sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like profit and dividend remittances and international travel," said economists at \uc2 Banco Santander\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Santander expects an August current account deficit of $4.9 billion and incoming foreign investment of $3.5 billion, leaving a still growing financing gap.\par \par \uc2 Though fundamentals in Brazil may require time to recover in a still slow global economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pledged to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrain volatility.\par \par \uc2 The bank Monday is scheduled to hold a daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap sale of $500 million as part of its plan to auction about $100 billion in swaps and dollar credit lines through the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, according to a weekly \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  market survey released Monday, the real is seen ending 2013 at BRL2.33 per dollar, and weakening through the end of 2014 to BRL2.40 per dollar. The same survey projected Brazil's IPCA consumer price index inflation to end the year at 5.81% and the benchmark Selic interest rate ending the year at 9.75%.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gerald Jeffris at gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 23-09-13 1259GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130923e99n000g1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
