\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  won't modify  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  program-  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2013\par 17:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Bank chief says \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  program is adequate, working well\par \par \uc2 * Delayed Fed tapering has been positive for EM assets\par \par \uc2 * Brazil ready to face U.S. monetary policy normalization\par \par \uc2 By Alonso Soto\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Sept 23 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its $60 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program unchanged despite a recent rally in the real supported by the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to continue pumping dollars into the world economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  said on Monday the program, which provides dollar liquidity through the sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and repurchase agreements, has been successful in reducing volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "From our perspective the program is adequate, is working well, so there is no news whatsoever from our side on this issue," said Tombini when asked in a teleconference with reporters and analysts if the program could be adapted.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Guido Mantega had said last week shortly after the Fed's surprise decision that Brazil could reduce the amount of resources earmarked for the daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the real gained ground. Analysts say his comments were aimed at preventing the real from strenghtening too much, which could hit local exporters.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move on Wednesday to continue its stimulus plan for now brought back investors that had fled emerging market countries on the expectation of higher interest rates in the United States. The Brazilian real strengthened about 3 percent that day alone.\par \par \uc2 Before that gain the real, which had slipped to its weakest level in nearly five years in August, was the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had weakened the most against the dollar in the previous six months.\par \par \uc2 After the real's August slump, Brazilian policymakers launched the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, which has been welcomed by many analysts on the grounds that it has provided markets with the predictability needed to halt the plunge of the real.\par \par \uc2 The weaker real has eroded the country's external accounts, pushed up inflationary pressures and increased risks to local companies with large dollar debts.\par \par \uc2 The real was trading 0.83 percent stronger at 2.1995 per dollar in the early afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Tombini said the country was ready to face the normalization of U.S. monetary policy but stressed that a gradual withdrawal of stimulus would be most beneficial for emerging-market nations and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 He said he was "cautiously optimistic" about the Brazilian economy.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/TOMBINI (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130923e99n000rg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
