\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  up as traders await Fed decision\b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2013\par 16:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 18 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on Wednesday as investors positioned for the outcome of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy-setting meeting, due later in the session. Brazil's real firmed 0.46 percent against the dollar while Mexico's peso edged lower. Chile's market was closed for a national holiday. The Fed is expected to announce a decision on whether to cut back its $85 billion-a-month bond-buying program, and by how much, at 1800 GMT on Wednesday. Bond buying by the Fed has provided a steady source of dollars seeking higher returns in emerging markets. Fears the bank would start cutting back on bond purchases have roiled most emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the real, leading \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to carry out daily market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank held an auction of traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps on Wednesday, followed up by a second auction designed to roll over expiring swap contracts.\par \par \uc2  "I think the auctions are helping fuel the strengthening of the real, especially at a moment when the market tends to operate with reduced liquidity in the wait of the Fed decision," said Luciano Rostagno, chief strategist with Banco Mizuho in Sao Paulo. The swaps are derivatives sold by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the futures market to provide investors with protection against the prospect of further depreciation of the real. Mexico's peso fell slightly to 12.933 per U.S. dollar, snapping a 10-day rally. "It's important to remember that, to the extent the Fed does tighten policy, it will be due to a strengthening US economy," wrote Neil Shearing, chief emerging markets economist with Capital Economics in an investor note. "This should support growth in many emerging markets - notably Mexico."\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     Key Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1354 GMT:\par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                daily %     YTD %\par                                            change    change\par                                  Latest            \par  Brazil real                     2.2474      0.46     -8.94\par                                                    \par  Mexico peso                    12.9330     -0.07     -0.53\par                                                    \par  Colombia peso                1912.0000      0.23     -7.64\par                                                    \par  Peru sol                        2.7700      0.00     -7.91\par                                                    \par  Argentina peso (interbank)      5.7425      0.00    -14.45\par                                                    \par  Argentina peso (parallel)       9.2900      0.22    -27.02\par                                                    \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Asher Levine and Marilia Carrera Editing by W Simon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | M | RBN | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130921e99i0005p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
