\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  Emerging Markets Hope to Get Some Attention at G-20, Too\b0\par\par\uc2 By PAUL HANNON and PAULO TREVISANI   \par \uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2013\par 08:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Leaders from the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  economies have been preoccupied by Syria ahead of their summit this week, but some are still hoping to find a way to steady the suddenly shaky fortunes of emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, most of the economic threats tackled by the G-20 have stemmed from large flows of capital from developed countries to developing ones in the wake of the 2008 financial crisis. But now that flow has begun to reverse, posing new challenges to the global recovery.\par \par \uc2 The turnaround has accelerated in recent weeks as investors anticipate an end to the U.S. Federal Reserve's period of extraordinary monetary stimulus this year. The result has been sharp falls in developing-country \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The central banks of three G-20 members-\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Indonesia and Turkey-have raised interest rates, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or both in recent weeks to try to stem the declines.\par \par \uc2 In India, where the rupee has suffered the most-precipitous decline among large Asian economies, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also taken the step of selling dollars to state-run oil refiners via a commercial bank to shift demand for dollars away from the open market.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2  on Tuesday warned that an already subdued recovery in global economic growth could be weakened further if the outflow of capital from developing economies intensifies.\par \par \uc2 Some leaders are hoping that the issue can still be addressed, even if the meetings in St. Petersburg on Thursday and Friday are dominated by the crisis surrounding the alleged use of chemical weapons in Syria's civil war.\par \par \uc2 For instance, a person familiar with Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff's thinking said she hopes the summit will produce agreement on ways to ease the impact of the anticipated change in Fed policy.\par \par \uc2 "The tone of her speech [at the summit] will show how upset she may be with U.S. policies," said a Brazilian government official.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian officials say they have the backing of other developing countries in seeking moves to address recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, although they are unclear about which among the G-20 members will be offering support. In particular, they want the Fed to improve the way it communicates its intentions to reduce market swings.\par \par \uc2 With the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  focusing on efforts to boost support for its approach to Syria, those developing economies may be left frustrated. But even without Syria, the U.S. would have been unlikely to make major concessions, economists say.\par \par \uc2 "Other than a vague commitment to take into account the effects of its monetary policy on other countries, the Fed is in practice likely to make no concessions to developments elsewhere," said Capital Economics, a London-based research firm.\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 04-09-13 0649GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020130904e9940007w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
