\par\par \b \uc2 Leaders Seek Balm for Emerging Markets at G-20 Summit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Hannon and Paulo Trevisani   \par \uc2 975 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2013\par 19:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Leaders from the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  economies have been preoccupied by Syria ahead of their summit this week, but some are still hoping to find a way to steady the suddenly shaky fortunes of emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, most of the economic threats tackled by the G-20 have stemmed from large flows of capital from developed countries to developing ones in the wake of the 2008 financial crisis. But now that flow has begun to reverse, posing new challenges to the global recovery.\par \par \uc2 The turnaround has accelerated in recent weeks as investors anticipate an end to the U.S. Federal Reserve's period of extraordinary monetary stimulus this year. The result has been sharp falls in developing-country \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The central banks of three G-20 members--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Indonesia and Turkey--have raised interest rates, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or both in recent weeks to try to stem the declines.\par \par \uc2 In India, where the rupee has suffered the most-precipitous decline among large Asian economies, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also taken the step of selling dollars to state-run oil refiners via a commercial bank to shift demand for dollars away from the open market.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2  on Tuesday warned that an already subdued recovery in global economic growth could be weakened further if the outflow of capital from developing economies intensifies.\par \par \uc2 Some leaders are hoping that the issue can still be addressed, even if the meetings in St. Petersburg on Thursday and Friday are dominated by the crisis surrounding the alleged use of chemical weapons in Syria's civil war.\par \par \uc2 For instance, a person familiar with Brazilian President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's thinking said she hopes the summit will produce agreement on ways to ease the impact of the anticipated change in Fed policy.\par \par \uc2 "The tone of her speech [at the summit] will show how upset she may be with U.S. policies," said a Brazilian government official.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian officials say they have the backing of other developing countries in seeking moves to address recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, although they are unclear about which among the G-20 members will be offering support. In particular, they want the Fed to improve the way it communicates its intentions to reduce market swings.\par \par \uc2 With the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  focusing on efforts to boost support for its approach to Syria, those developing economies may be left frustrated. But even without Syria, the U.S. would have been unlikely to make major concessions, economists say.\par \par \uc2 "Other than a vague commitment to take into account the effects of its monetary policy on other countries, the Fed is in practice likely to make no concessions to developments elsewhere," said Capital Economics, a London-based research firm.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to exercise more control over their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the governments of Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa have been working on a joint proposal to set up a $100 billion Contingency Reserve Arrangement by pooling \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 Leaders may make some progress in St. Petersburg, since that doesn't require input from the U.S. or other developed countries, although the new fund is unlikely to be launched until next year.\par \par \uc2 In March, finance ministers of the five "Brics" countries signed an agreement to kick-start the arrangement during their annual meeting in South Africa. China will contribute $41 billion, followed by Brazil, Russia and India with $18 billion each, and South Africa with $5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The focus on Syria will also probably mean that developed-country members of the G-20 pay less attention to a long-running dispute over whether to set binding deadlines for stabilizing their budget deficits and beginning to cut their debts.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, German officials have pushed the G-20 nations to agree to hard targets for reducing debt after the current deficit-cutting agreement expires in 2016. But the effort ran into strong U.S. opposition when finance ministers met in Moscow in July, with Treasury Department officials insisting that the priority had to be boosting jobs and growth as opposed to reducing debt.\par \par \uc2 Rather than a hard target, the G-20 countries are expected to lay out "medium-term fiscal strategies" to show how they will achieve "sustainable growth" after 2016, a German official told reporters in Berlin on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "This is better than no strategy, but these aren't binding targets," the official said.\par \par \uc2 G-20 leaders are also set to produce a plan for supporting growth and creating jobs.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. administration official said an overriding U.S. goal at the summit is persuading other nations that they need to help boost demand world-wide. Global demand is "improving," but "remains weak," the official said.\par \par \uc2 "We need to rededicate ourselves to promoting a lasting rebalancing of global demand," the official said. "We welcome signs that Europe's long recession is ending and their critical steps to restore financial stability. But important challenges remain, including boosting demand and addressing record-high unemployment levels."\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, the U.S. wants to make progress toward another goal: cracking down on tax evasion.\par \par \uc2 That issue was front-and-center at a separate meeting of the \uc2 Group of Eight\uc2  nations in Northern Ireland in June.\par \par \uc2 "We will also be working in St. Petersburg to get support globally for the kinds of measures that we have laid out for countries to close tax loopholes and avoid a race to the bottom where tax competition leads countries to lose revenue and companies to make inefficient decisions when they locate where they pay the lowest tax, rather than where it's most productive for them to produce," an Obama administration official said.\par \par \uc2 --Peter Nicholas and Anton Troianovski contributed to this article. [ 09-03-13 1333ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020130903e993000du\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
