\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Brazil hikes interest rates again to regain market confidence\b0\par\par\uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2013\par 02:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hikes Selic rate by 50 bps as expected\par \par \uc2 * Weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adds to inflationary pressures\par \par \uc2 * Bank leaves door open for more rate hikes   (Adds analyst comment and context)\par \par \uc2 By Anthony Boadle and Alonso Soto\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Aug 28 (Reuters) - Brazil raised its benchmark interest rate to a 16-month high of 9 percent on Wednesday, maintaining the pace of monetary tightening to fight inflation and rebuild investors' confidence in Latin America's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee, known as Copom, voted unanimously for a third straight half-percentage-point rate hike to ease inflationary fears that have risen on the back of a plunge in the value of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Forty-four of 46 economists polled by Reuters expected the bank to raise its Selic rate to 9 percent from 8.5 percent as part of a tightening cycle that started in April.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left the door open for more rate hikes by reiterating that the latest increase is part of a rate-adjustment process.\par \par \uc2 "Continuing the adjustment of the benchmark interest rate, Copom decided unanimously to raise the Selic rate to 9 percent," said the bank in its post-decision statement that repeated exactly the language  of the last communique in July.\par \par \uc2 Monetary authorities from India to Indonesia are scrambling to offset the massive outflow of capital triggered by expectations of a withdrawal of stimulus in the United States and a stronger dollar. The exodus of investors have dragged down the value of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most emerging-market nations, pushing inflation higher via pricier imports.\par \par \uc2 The stakes are even higher for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  where a mix of erratic policy that includes government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in sectors of the economy plus supply-side bottlenecks have severely hit investor confidence in the once-booming emerging-market nation.\par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates would help Brazil not only tame price increases, but also bolster investors' confidence, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said in a report about the South American nation published earlier on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The double-digit slump of the real had even prompted some market bets for a larger rate hike on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  threw cold water on those expectations by saying on Aug. 19 that the steep rise in the yield of interest rate future contracts was excessive. The bank also launched a $60 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program to halt the plunge of the real, which has been one of the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world this year.\par \par \uc2 The scope of future rate hikes will likely hinge on the pace of depreciation of the real as well as the strength of the economic recovery in Brazil, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "I think... (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will follow the data and decide accordingly. There will be at least another 50 basis points that will come in mid-October and we could see things heading on their way to 10 (percent) afterwards," said Jose Francisco de Lima Goncalves, chief economist with \uc2 Banco Fator\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 TEXT-Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision statement\par \par \uc2 INSTANT VIEW on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate hike\par \par \uc2 INSIGHT-Brazil's Tombini: An inflation hawk\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has argued that high inflation ultimately hurts the economy by discouraging investment in the country and domestic spending by local businesses and consumers.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian economy likely rebounded in the second quarter, but recent data show that the recovery will again disappoint with growth of just 2 percent this year. Brazil's statistics institute will release second-quarter gross domestic product figures at 9 a.m. (1200 GMT) on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ERRATIC POLICY\par \par \uc2 The bank started to raise the Selic in April after a spike in consumer prices, surprising investors with the aggressive pace of tightening that brought rates from record lows of 7.25 percent to 8.50 percent in July.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation has eased since then, but at 6.27 percent remains well above the mid-point of the official year-end target of 4.5 percent, plus or minus two percentage points. Inflation expectations also remain high for next year.\par \par \uc2 A possible hike in gasoline prices in Brazil expected later this year could also fuel inflation by raising transport costs.\par \par \uc2 The inflation problem partly stems from a flurry of consumption-stimulus packages implemented by President Dilma Rousseff's government, which also raised tensions between her administration and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts have said.\par \par \uc2 The government's aggressive spending is seen as one of the main reasons why the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to boost rates this year, even as the economy struggles to find its footing.\par \par \uc2 The likely normalization of monetary policy in the United States has added to inflationary concerns in Brazil, a country ravaged by bouts of hyperinflation in the 1980s and 1990s. Although analysts do not expect inflation to get out of control, authorities are under pressure to contain any surge in prices.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Guido Mantega has blamed the Fed for fueling a "mini-crisis" with mixed messages - about winding down or not, its liquidity expansion program (quantitative easing).\par \par \uc2 Adding to the uncertainty in markets is a likely military strike by the United States and allies on the Syrian government, which is accused of using chemical weapons against civilians.   (Additional reporting by Luciana Otoni, Asher Levine and Silvio Cascione; Editing by Andrew Hay and Carol Bishopric)\par \par \uc2 HOLD - BRAZIL-ECONOMY/RATES (UPDATE 3) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | ABX | BNX | FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130901e98t00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
