\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Despite Support, Brazil Real Seen Volatile Until Fed Clarity\b0\par\par\uc2 1,354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2013\par 20:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil's real will continue to show some volatility despite local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions as the market awaits clearer signals from the US Federal Reserve on its monetary policy, says Joao Paulo Correa, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at the Correparti brokerage in Curitiba. "The market has a lot of expectations about the Fed, but we don't fully know yet," he says, adding \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have limited impact in boosting the real. His firm sees the real trading within BRL2.35 to BRL2.45 per dollar--around its weakest in four years--until investors get a firmer grasp on the Fed's intentions. Dollar last at BRL2.3822, up 1.4%. (gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:26 EDT - Mexico's July trade numbers came as a breath of fresh air after last week's disappointing 2Q GDP, with exports up 6.3% on the year and imports up 9.6%, both well above the average growth for the first seven months of the year. "The numbers point to a good performance in domestic demand, good prospects for investment, and a recovery in industrial production in July," the Vector brokerage says. July's trade balance was a deficit of $1.44B. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The cost to insure Argentine sovereign debt against default ticked higher as investors continue to digest the latest development in the country's legal battle with holdout creditors. A US appeals court ruled against Argentina Friday, saying the country must pay bondholders who held out on a restructuring offer years ago if it continues to pay those who accepted the deal. Now, investors are waiting to see if the Supreme Court will weigh in on Argentina's case. If the Supreme Court refuses to hear the case, that significantly raises the risks of default, analysts say. The spread on Argentina's 5-year credit default swaps trades at 2,512bps, from 2,433bps late Friday, according to Markit. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:44 EDT - In recent years, worries that dented emerging-market assets usually meant haven flows into Treasurys. But this year, concerns about less Fed stimulus mean outflows from EM are actually hurting demand for Treasurys. "We could, at least temporarily, be stuck in a downward spiral," Nomura says, pointing to reserve losses in developing countries selling dollars to prop up their struggling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Firm estimates about 35%-40% of EM reserves are invested in Treasury notes and bonds. With many now \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nomura estimates waning reserves will reduce demand for Treasurys from EM central banks by about $20B-$25B each month. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Or so \uc2 UBS\uc2  thinks. The Treasury primary dealer says "injecting some uncertainty" into the QE exit plan could help mitigate sharp reactions in financial markets, especially when higher mortgage rates are weighing on the housing recovery. Specifically, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says the Fed should give one lump sum of what it's purchasing in Treasurys and MBS instead of delineating how much it's buying in each category. That way, it can have more flexibility month to month, and could even increase purchases in one group while decreasing overall purchases. For now, the firm expects the Fed to announce tapering in September, reducing $10B in monthly purchases split between Treasurys and MBS. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Peru's consumer price index may post an annual rise of 3.1% or 3.2% through the end of August, banks forecast, from 3.24% in July. \uc2 UBS\uc2  cites a slowdown in domestic demand and relatively stable commodities prices as part of why inflation should come back down toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range of 1.0% to 3.0%. "Inflation will probably decelerate after a bad July number, gradually converging to the target, helped by an improvement in food supply," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says, forecasting a 12-month increase of 3.2%. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Analysts were widely expecting \uc2 Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates on hold at 1.25%, giving the shekel little cause to move in either direction in response to such a move. For now, markets will likely be focused on who will be selected as the next governor of the Bank of Israel. While the shekel has weakened some this month, \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  says watch for the dollar to make a clear break above ILS3.60. After that, the pair could target ILS3.62 and then 3.65. The dollar is currently up 0.6% on today's durable-goods data at ISR3.606. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy)\par \par \uc2 The July durable-goods report is just one of a series of bad prints lately that may put a September rollback of the Fed's bond-buying program in jeopardy, says \uc2 Douglas Borthwick\uc2  of Chapdelaine \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "This is not a positive development. The weaker US data continues to throw a wrench into any and all tapering decisions, with the likely outcome being a postponement of tapering until the US economy shows either signs of bottoming or signs of steady improvement," Borthwick says. Of course the payrolls report due September 6 is still the one to watch. A solid jobs number would do much to bolster the crumbling argument for imminent tapering. USD/JPY at 98.38, down 0.3%. EUR/USD turns positive, at 1.3388. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 July's durable-goods report echoes a recent theme: bad, but not bad enough to derail September tapering expectations. Treasurys bounce to session highs, though yields remain near 2-year highs, with the 10-year up 6/32 to yield 2.794%. Meanwhile, the dollar slips to Y98.325 from Y98.515 just before the release and the euro rises to $1.3386 from $1.3369. Perhaps not the kind of economic signal the Fed wants to see before pulling the tapering trigger, but the key still rests in the forthcoming payrolls report. A bad result there is needed to cast real doubt on an autumn taper. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 A 52% slump in volatile airplane demand triggers a larger-than-expected 7.3% decline in July durable-goods orders. The bigger news is that nondefense, nonplane capital-goods orders posted a 3.3% slide after five monthly gains. Higher interest rates may have caused some pullback on the part of businesses placing orders for big-ticket items. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Overnight Treasurys activity slowed to a trickle, with volumes at a mere 18% of the 10-day moving average, according to CRT Capital. That's the lowest since Dec. 31, and while it's not surprising for the last week of August, there's plenty to digest for those who are around. As such, that may lead to exaggerated price swings. The Treasury's $98B supply this week will weigh on the market, though month-end demand should offset that. Meanwhile, a variety of economic data this week will tug the market back and forth ahead of the September FOMC meeting, widely expected to deliver a QE-tapering announcement. Benchmark 10-year notes are off 2/32, yielding 2.828%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EDT - After a very mixed weak for the major US stock indexes (the \uc2 Dow\uc2  industrials fell 0.5% while the Nasdaq Composite rose 1.5%), futures--among other US markets--are little changed to start the week. Volume is liable to continue plumbing the year's lows this week in the run-up to Labor Day and the end of summer, though there's a fair amount of economic data to drive a bit of action. On tap today is July durable goods and August's \uc2 Dallas Fed\uc2  reading. S&P 500 futures are down 0.5 point as Nymex oil edges higher, gold is barely in the red and Treasurys are flat. The euro also eases a touch, trading around $1.3375. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com) [ 26-08-13 1839GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130826e98q000di\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
