\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Despite Support, Brazil Real Seen Volatile Until Fed Clarity\b0\par\par\uc2 1,482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2013\par 20:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil's real will continue to show some volatility despite local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions as the market awaits clearer signals from the US Federal Reserve on its monetary policy, says Joao Paulo Correa, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at the Correparti brokerage in Curitiba. "The market has a lot of expectations about the Fed, but we don't fully know yet," he says, adding \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have limited impact in boosting the real. His firm sees the real trading within BRL2.35 to BRL2.45 per dollar--around its weakest in four years--until investors get a firmer grasp on the Fed's intentions. Dollar last at BRL2.3822, up 1.4%. (gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 14:23 EDT - Despite mixed economic signals out of China, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  (BA) and Xiamen Airlines finalized an order for six 787-8 Dreamliners, in a move to bolster its international routes. The order, first announced in May 2011, is valued at $1.3B at list prices, but customers typically receive discounts. Heavy pressure on passenger yields aboard Chinese airlines have not dented a steady stream of deliveries from BA and Airbus, a unit of \uc2 European Aeronautic Defence & Space Co\uc2 . Xiamen is the only all-\uc2 Boeing\uc2  Chinese carrier, but the airline is a customer for the Comac ARJ21, a domestically-built-- and heavily delayed--70-90 seat regional jet. Separately, BA's first larger 787-9 left the factory Saturday as it prepares for its first test flight later this summer. BA gains 0.8% to $106.36. (jon.ostrower@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:21 EDT - Lightstream Resources (LTS.T) could be ripe for a shake-up by an activist investor, BMO suggested, after the oil & gas exploration company posted another weak quarter and shares hit a 52-week low earlier this month. Under the headline, "Is There Light at the End of This Tunnel? Activists Wanted," BMO suggests LTS.T consider a dividend cut and asset sales or change in strategic direction to reignite its stock price. Shares down 0.7% at C$7.35, and have steadily decreased since October 2009 when they traded above C$40. A spokesman wasn't immediately available for comment. (ben.dummett@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:07 EDT - "Since the Fed raised the prospect of a modest shift in policy, money has poured out of emerging market countries causing severe volatility and economic upheaval," notes Baird strategist Bruce Bittles. "Considering the multitude of moving parts, we are assuming that the Fed will either maintain the current rate of bond purchases at $85 billion a month or offer a token withdrawal of just $10 billion," he says. "The equity markets are expected look favorably on this outcome." Attention will turn to second look at 2Q GDP Thursday. Growth below 2% would likely stay the Fed's hand on September tapering, Bittles thinks, while something above 2.5% may mean the Fed reduces bond purchases "very slowly." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:00 EDT - Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to offer some $60B in dollar swaps and loans through the end of the year will help limit excess speculation and volatility, but won't eliminate all pressure against the real, says analyst Reginaldo Galhardo at the Treviso brokerage in Sao Paulo. "The actions of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should trim some of the speculative edge in the market, but they don't change the prospects for broader economic conditions that have been influencing investors," he says. Brazil's real is more than 1% weaker against the dollar Monday following the first of a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions scheduled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the end of the year. (gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The cost to insure Argentine sovereign debt against default ticked higher as investors continue to digest the latest development in the country's legal battle with holdout creditors. A US appeals court ruled against Argentina Friday, saying the country must pay bondholders who held out on a restructuring offer years ago if it continues to pay those who accepted the deal. Now, investors are waiting to see if the Supreme Court will weigh in on Argentina's case. If the Supreme Court refuses to hear the case, that significantly raises the risks of default, analysts say. The spread on Argentina's 5-year credit default swaps trades at 2,512bps, from 2,433bps late Friday, according to Markit. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:44 EDT - In recent years, worries that dented emerging-market assets usually meant haven flows into Treasurys. But this year, concerns about less Fed stimulus mean outflows from EM are actually hurting demand for Treasurys. "We could, at least temporarily, be stuck in a downward spiral," Nomura says, pointing to reserve losses in developing countries selling dollars to prop up their struggling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Firm estimates about 35%-40% of EM reserves are invested in Treasury notes and bonds. With many now \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nomura estimates waning reserves will reduce demand for Treasurys from EM central banks by about $20B-$25B each month. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 11:39 EDT - In the absence of support from overseas markets, US traders were happy to sell into this morning's copper rally. Futures took a dive as US equities opened, turning a 0.6% gain into a loss of more than 1% in the space of half an hour. With traders in London's metal hub away for a bank holiday (and global benchmark London Metal Exchange closed), readings on the health of demand globally were hard to come by. That and weak US durable goods data pushed traders to sell futures that had been creeping toward a near-three-month high. Comex September copper off 1% at $3.3135 a pound. (matt.day@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:34 EDT - Citi thinks 3D printing is "on the cusp of seeing much broader adoption" as it starts coverage of 3D-printer makers 3D Systems (DDD) and Stratasys (SSYS) with a pair of buys. The investment bank says DDD's product line "is best positioned to capitalize on all three potential market opportunities (prototyping, manufacturing, consumer)," as it sets a $60 price target. SSYS, gets a $125 price target from Citi, which sees "sustainable margin expansion and earnings growth with materials consumption driving recurring revenue mix." Volatile DDD jumps 9.1% to $52.87 and SSYS rises 6.1% to $112.25. (anna.prior@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 EDT - Bristow Group (BRS) down 2% at $65.97 as the largest listed helicopter operator digests the potential impact of the grounding of some Super Puma helicopters following a fatal crash involving oil workers being transported from a North Sea platform by rival \uc2 CHC Helicopter\uc2 . Houston-based Bristow grounded as a precaution its only two \uc2 Eurocopter\uc2  Super Puma L2 helicopters--both in Nigeria--having cancelled some flights operated with other aircraft in the U.K. over the weekend. Operators generate the bulk of revenues from standing charges paid regardless of whether the helicopters are flying. EADS, parent of \uc2 Eurocopter\uc2 , recently down 0.1% at EUR44.41, in line with the local Paris market. (doug.cameron@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Peru's consumer price index may post an annual rise of 3.1% or 3.2% through the end of August, banks forecast, from 3.24% in July. \uc2 UBS\uc2  cites a slowdown in domestic demand and relatively stable commodities prices as part of why inflation should come back down toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range of 1.0% to 3.0%. "Inflation will probably decelerate after a bad July number, gradually converging to the target, helped by an improvement in food supply," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says, forecasting a 12-month increase of 3.2%. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Analysts were widely expecting \uc2 Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates on hold at 1.25%, giving the shekel little cause to move in either direction in response to such a move. For now, markets will likely be focused on who will be selected as the next governor of the Bank of Israel. While the shekel has weakened some this month, \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  says watch for the dollar to make a clear break above ILS3.60. After that, the pair could target ILS3.62 and then 3.65. The dollar is currently up 0.6% on today's durable-goods data at ISR3.606. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 26, 2013 14:39 ET (18:39 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130826e98q003px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
