\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Stocks Climb on  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Currency Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2013\par 22:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazilian blue-chip stocks climbed Friday, led by homebuilder and retail stocks, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set out its longer-term strategy for reining in the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness.\par \par \uc2 The Ibovespa index of most-traded stocks rose 1.6% to 52197 points, for a weekly advance of 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said late Thursday that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to ensure investors and companies had access to U.S. dollars. The move, which will give more clarity and predictability to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans, caused the Brazilian real to strengthen sharply against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Because the real's weakness against the dollar boosts the cost of imports and a result increases inflationary pressures, some traders saw the move as helping keep down interest rates in the medium term. Last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey, the most recent one published, shows that the market expects the benchmark Selic interest rate to climb from 8.5% currently to 9.25% by year end, and then to 9.5% by the end of 2014.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the long end of the rate curve dipped somewhat, reflecting a changing outlook for inflation. With some expectations that rates may climb less than previously forecast, stocks of companies more dependent on consumer credit jumped.\par \par \uc2 Real estate developer Rossi Residencial surged 9.5% to three reais ($1.27), leading gains on the index. Rival \uc2 Gafisa SA\uc2  added 8.4% to BRL3.10, while homebuilder PDG Realty SA gained 8% to BRL2.43, and \uc2 Brookfield Incorporacoes\uc2  rose 6.2% to BRL1.88.\par \par \uc2 Leading online retailer B2W jumped 7.2% to BRL13.51, while clothing company \uc2 Cia. Hering SA\uc2  climbed 5.3% to BRL 31.90, and retailer \uc2 Lojas Renner SA\uc2  advanced 4.1% to BRL60.62.\par \par \uc2 The stronger real also lifted shares of state-run oil company \uc2 Petroleo Brasileiro SA\uc2 , which rose 1.6% to BRL18.55. The real's recent weakness against the dollar had increased the cost of gasoline and diesel the company imports to meet domestic fuel demand.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Eike Batista\uc2 's \uc2 EBX Group\uc2  of companies also were gainers during the session. Oil company OGX advanced 5.2% to BRL0.81, while mining company MMX added 4.9% to BRL2.35.\par \par \uc2 But just as the stronger real favors importing companies, it didn't favor exporters such as mining company \uc2 Vale SA\uc2 , one of Brazil's largest exporters. Vale shares slipped 0.4% to BRL32.28.\par \par \uc2 Aircraft manufacturer Embraer fell 1.8% to BRL19.81, while \uc2 Suzano Papel e Celulose\uc2  sank 2% to BRL8.87.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Winterstein at paulo.winterstein@wsj.com [ 23-08-13 2050GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1843 5053   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130823e98n000ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
