\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Brazilian Rate-Futures Yields Drop on  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2013\par 18:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Yields on \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest-rate futures have fallen sharply on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $60B \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program as investors bet on less inflationary pressure coming from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The benchmark overnight rate is now seen at 9.18% in January, down 6 basis points from Thursday's close, while the expected rate in January 2015 fell 23bp to 10.33%. "The US markets being calm today has helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action have an impact," says Paulo Petrassi, a fund manager at Leme Investimentos. "Interest rates had gotten very high and were totally overshooting." (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 We may get a rollback of the Fed's bond-buying program in September, but don't expect the dollar to go gangbusters on the news, says TD's Millan Millrane. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has pretty much digested the fact that tapering is not tightening. "Even if they do scale back their purchases, the Fed would still be expanding their balance sheet." He notes the growing acceptance of that view has reigned in the dollar, keeping it in a tight range against the yen and euro despite a belief that tapering is imminent. But Millrane says the dollar still has a chance to rally if tapering's scale is larger than expected. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to hold steady, daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through year-end is stabilizing the real, but the Fed will still determine the fate of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Ilan Solot\uc2  of \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . Calling this week's meeting minutes "inconclusive," he notes EM units like the real could get pummeled if the Fed makes good on tapering and the 10-year yield moves back toward or above 3%. "Everyone is going to get hit again." But Solot says the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would benefit if the Fed delays tapering or reduces bond purchases less than consensus. "There is plenty of room for disappointment" from whatever option the Fed selects." (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics says Canada's housing market is likely headed for a multi-year period of below-trend growth, but not a crash that some forecast. It says home sales have begun to slip and price growth is slowing, "in part because of government efforts to discourage debt-fueled property speculation." \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says those tighter mortgage financing rules have cut demand at the high end, with notable effect in expensive markets such as Vancouver. "At the same time, elevated price-to-rent ratios have tempered much of the enthusiasm that drove home buyers in recent years," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  adds. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 A big drop in new home sales forcing anti-Treasurys bets to unwind. The short covering sent the 10-year yield to a session low of 2.802%. But some traders caution that new home sales has been a volatile gauge of the housing market. The bond market needs persistently weak data to reverse the rout since May. Andrew Brenner of \uc2 National Alliance\uc2  Capital Markets still sees the 10-year yield hitting 3% "sooner rather than later," the key datapoint being the September 6 jobs report. Larry Dyer, head of US interest rate strategy at \uc2 HSBC Securities USA\uc2 , questions the strength of 2H growth and bets the yield will end the year down around 2.6%. Ten-year notes up 20/32, yielding 2.818%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to hold daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the end of 2013 will help end the "feeding frenzy" surrounding the real, says Gradual Investimentos. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "wants to give companies that are being punished by the US dollar's appreciation a way out," firm says. Gradual notes dollar-denominated obligations held by companies are at $180B, up from $100B during the 2008 financial crisis. The goal is not to strengthen the real specifically to BRL2.20 per dollar, but to end speculation that it could weaken to BRL2.75, Gradual adds. BRL holding gains, with the dollar down 2.3% to BRL2.379. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Argentine bonds are mostly higher Friday despite a major ruling against the country in favor of holdout creditors. A US federal appeals court ruled that Argentina can't pay bonds it issued under a swap deal unless it pays the holdouts full value at the same time, but left a suspension of enforcement in place while the Supreme Court considers an appeal. The NY law, dollar-denominated 2017 bond is up 0.1% at ARS725. The NY law dollar-GDP warrants rose 0.1% to ARS71.00. (shane.romig@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 That's not the way economists want to see housing supply increase. Based on July figures, it'd take 5.2 months to buy up the supply of newly built homes on the market -- the most in at least a year. But economists note that builders are still highly sensitive to demand, cautious about ramping up supply too quickly despite the improvements seen this year. Already, homes that broke ground in July reported a week ago came in at the slowest pace in eight months. Look for builders to clamp down further on starts this month to catch up with waning demand. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Even in today's rebounding housing market, there's not a ton of new homes being sold each month, so it would stand to reason it's not that hard to get good reads--even initially--on activity. That's what makes the size of June's revision, cutting the annualized sales rate for the month 8.5% from initial data a month ago, so shocking. On an unadjusted basis, the Commerce Department said in July that 48K new homes were sold in June. Today, that number has been cut to 43K, with the biggest change coming in the South's numbers. And May's total has been dropped yet again, from 45K initially to 43K last month to 41K in today's report. July's initial number is 35K; what's the chance that doesn't get revised notably? (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The uneven nature of the US recovery is making it hard to get a sustained dollar rally going. Case in point: after several consecutive data prints that showed an economy making progress, we get a number like today's July new home sales, which tumbled to a 9-month low. That's one reason why USD/JPY remains under Y100, and EUR/USD seems buoyant around $1.33, despite widespread belief that the Federal Reserve will begin tapering its bond buying program next month. Investors want to have faith, but the data undermines it every now and then. USD/JPY down to 98.86 from 99.02 before the data. EUR/USD up to 1.3370 from 1.3350. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 July sales of new homes more than gave back what was originally reported as a sharp rise in June sales. The 13.4% fall comes on top of June sales being revised lower to 455K from 497K. The magnitude of the drop may be a bit surprising though economists widely expected a pullback as mortgage rates climbed in July, purchase applications receded a bit and building permits declined. It'll take a few more months of waning home sales to cause real concern about higher borrowing costs damaging the housing recovery. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Rising bond yields show signs of hurting the housing markets with the ugly July new-home-sales report, and further softness in housing data could raise concerns from the Fed--possibly to the point of delaying the taper. In that case, bond bears may cut back short bets and keep a lid on further gains in the 10-year yield. The note is 6/32 higher, yielding 2.874%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 12:55 p.m. EDT because the original version incorrectly contained pricing data for April 2014 and 2015 instead of January of those years. Yields are now seen being 9.18% in January, down 6 basis points from Thursday's close, while the expected rate in January 2015 fell 23bp to 10.33%. [ 23-08-13 1642GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130823e98n000ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
