\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Treasurys Ticking Up as Bargain Hunters Step In\b0\par\par\uc2 1,327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2013\par 15:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Bond prices are ticking higher as yields near two-year highs bring in bargain hunters. The market is still vulnerable to selling pressure, but the 10-year yielding near 3% makes it more attractive compared to when it traded below 2% three months ago. With the inflation rate below 2%, buyers can actually now eke out positive inflation-adjusted returns. The higher the 10-year yield rises, it will woo more buyers who remain skeptical about the growth outlook. Fed's Bullard also gave buyers encouragement. Bullard, who votes on interest-rate policy this year, told CNBC that he doesn't think the Fed is in a hurry to taper. The 10-year note is recently 1/32 higher, yielding 2.888%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds showed only a modest upwards quiver after July CPI came in a tick lower than expected. While the data do show inflation that's perking up a little after barely showing a pulse in recent months, it remains well below the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's 2% target and broadly consistent with its forecasts. The bottom line is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to remain sidelined despite its mildly hawkish rhetoric, a stark contrast with the situation in the US. Canada's 2-year benchmark debt yields 1.212%, versus 1.221% pre-data, while the greenback edges down to C$1.0551 from C$1.0562; it was at C$1.0517 late Thursday. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Quiet start for the last session of the week. Bond prices are flat after two-day selling with 10-year yield near two-year high. Anxiety over Fed tapering remains the main driver for yields. Stronger data would push the yield higher and could test 3% for the first time since July 2011. July new home sales due at 10:00am ET. The 10-year note recently flat, yielding 2.896%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $60B \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program "should help contain excessive bearishness" and put the real in a BRL2.35-40 range versus the dollar for a spell, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . "But the measure should not reverse the weakening trend of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been driven by changes in US monetary-policy stance and uncertainty around local fiscal policy." The real has gained sharply today against the greenback, sitting currently at BRL2.3825. (paul.kiernan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 With Canada CPI for July poised to again show relative price softness, the numbers are expected to be above the rock-bottom inflation readings seen earlier this year and signal that the trend is up, not down. Softer-than-expected numbers today should add some pressure to the loonie, which continues to grind lower in the wake of stronger greenback support. As such, the overnight high of C$1.0569 could be tested; the dollar is up 0.3% at C$1.0553, according to CQG. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com, @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 The $60B \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program announced late Thursday by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  represents one of the boldest attempts yet by an emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the rout of recent weeks, says Capital Economics. "Under the new program, Brazil will sell as much \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in one month as \uc2 India's Reserve Bank\uc2  has sold in the past four." The researcher also noted a price-trend reversal isn't likely as "history suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment rather than prevent the adjustment from happening altogether. If sentiment continues to turn against EMs over the coming weeks and months, it seems likely that the real will fall further." (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1004 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is using its heavy artillery to restrain depreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to Capital Economics. The $60 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced late last night represents one of the boldest attempts yet by an emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it says. "Brazil will sell as much \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in one month as \uc2 India's Reserve Bank\uc2  has sold in the past four." "History suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment rather than prevent the adjustment from happening altogether. If sentiment continues to turn against EMs over the coming weeks and months it seems likely that the real will fall further." (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's July industrial output rebounds after five months of declines, indicating improving confidence in the manufacturing sector and boding well for 3Q GDP growth, says Standard Chartered Bank economist Tony Phoo. July industrial output rose 2.07% on year after falling 0.40% in June; the reading is sharply higher than the median 0.85% decrease forecast by economists. "The above-view output figure not only reflects better exports but also suggests more active domestic economic activities," Phoo says. That's positive for third-quarter economic growth, he says. He forecasts 3Q GDP growth at 3.50%-4.00% on year, higher than the government's 2.47% growth forecast. The government says new product launches and China's upcoming week-long holiday in October will likely rejuvenate Taiwan's electronic component supply chain, but due to a high-base effect, the industrial-output growth rate might remain stagnant. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD falls slightly in thin volume, weighed by continued export settlements while also reflecting the weaker USD/KRW, say two local traders. The pair is at 30.053 vs 30.127 late Thursday in Taipei; they say the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying around 29.970 to keep the pair in its target band. "The U.S. dollar is unlikely to fall much as foreign-fund outflows are still providing support," a trader says. They tip the pair to trade in a 29.950-30.000 band Monday. Taiwan shares end 0.8% higher, with foreign investors net buyers of NT$728.4 million worth of local shares. Taiwan government bonds are steady in quiet trade, with the most-traded five-year bond yield at 1.1672% vs 1.1690% late Thursday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The USD/KRW is lower at 1,116.90 late Friday in Seoul vs 1,123.00 late Thursday on rising stocks and easing concern over the slowing of the U.S. Federal Reserve's stimulus, analysts say. "The Kospi's 1.1% rise on foreign buying provided support for the won," Samsung Futures \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Jeon Seung-ji says. "The local pair was largely in line with the slower pace of Asian emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling against the dollar." Local exporters' USD-selling for settlements also pushes the local pair lower. Ms. Jeon tips the USD/KRW in a band of 1,110-1,127 next week. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Singapore's inflation likely edged higher to 1.9% in July from a year earlier, vs 1.8% in June, as food and car prices rose, according to the median estimate in a \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  poll of seven analysts. The government will release July CPI data at 0500 GMT Friday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The NZD/USD is still under pressure and "seems to be basically ignoring some of the improved economic data we saw from China and the U.S.," says \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions Corporate Dealing Manager Chris Hunter. "The U.S. dollar bulls have well and truly taken yesterday morning's Fed minutes and run with them as far as they can," he says. Hunter tips the Kiwi to remain under pressure as long as the market continues to expect the Fed to begin tapering at the September meeting. The pair is at 0.7833 with Hunter pegging support at 0.7810 and resistance at 0.7860. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com) [ 23-08-13 1312GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130823e98n000e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
