\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Brazilian Rate-Futures Yields Drop on  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2013\par 18:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Yields on \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest-rate futures have fallen sharply on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $60B \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program as investors bet on less inflationary pressure coming from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The benchmark overnight rate is now seen at 9.18% in January, down 6 basis points from Thursday's close, while the expected rate in January 2015 fell 23bp to 10.33%. "The US markets being calm today has helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action have an impact," says Paulo Petrassi, a fund manager at Leme Investimentos. "Interest rates had gotten very high and were totally overshooting." (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:32 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says commodities markets risk becoming too euphoric as supply issues and extreme short positioning have driven recent price gains. The investment bank thinks without a significant improvement in global growth--of which there are only tentative signs at present--upward price trends in oil and copper are likely to falter. Supply shortfalls in oil are largely priced in at current levels for both the WTI and Brent benchmarks while copper is fairly priced and could decline to $6,683/ton in December--10% below current levels--says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . For precious metals, it thinks physical-demand support may be limited by the recent import duty and regulatory changes in India. (diana.kinch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:28 EDT - Kirkland's (KIRK) is building on Thursday's 11% pop, hitting a 3-year high today as Wedbush upgrades the home-decor retailer to outperform and hikes its price target to $22 from $16, with it saying KIRK's boosted FY guidance "looks conservative." The investment bank also calls the company "a viable competitor in home furnishings and accessories, with a well-differentiated merchandising strategy and significant organic-growth opportunities." The mall chain's sales have been soft for years, prompting a number of store closures over time. But Wedbush thinks KIRK could ultimately double from the current 300+ stores. Shares rise 5.6% to $19.51. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 We may get a rollback of the Fed's bond-buying program in September, but don't expect the dollar to go gangbusters on the news, says TD's Millan Millrane. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has pretty much digested the fact that tapering is not tightening. "Even if they do scale back their purchases, the Fed would still be expanding their balance sheet." He notes the growing acceptance of that view has reigned in the dollar, keeping it in a tight range against the yen and euro despite a belief that tapering is imminent. But Millrane says the dollar still has a chance to rally if tapering's scale is larger than expected. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:53 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  notes big drop in July new-home sales "stands in contrast to homebuilder sentiment, which has continued to rise in recent months, but is more in line with the decline in MBA mortgage applications for purchase," which fell 6.6% in July. The firm also notes new-home sales are based on signed contracts, "and we would expect any weakening in the housing market to show up here first, as opposed to in existing home sales which are recorded at the time of closing and, therefore, tend to lag changes in sentiment and activity by several months." July pending home sales Wednesday will also add more color. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics says Canada's housing market is likely headed for a multi-year period of below-trend growth, but not a crash that some forecast. It says home sales have begun to slip and price growth is slowing, "in part because of government efforts to discourage debt-fueled property speculation." \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says those tighter mortgage financing rules have cut demand at the high end, with notable effect in expensive markets such as Vancouver. "At the same time, elevated price-to-rent ratios have tempered much of the enthusiasm that drove home buyers in recent years," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  adds. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 A big drop in new home sales forcing anti-Treasurys bets to unwind. The short covering sent the 10-year yield to a session low of 2.802%. But some traders caution that new home sales has been a volatile gauge of the housing market. The bond market needs persistently weak data to reverse the rout since May. Andrew Brenner of National Alliance Capital Markets still sees the 10-year yield hitting 3% "sooner rather than later," the key datapoint being the September 6 jobs report. Larry Dyer, head of US interest rate strategy at \uc2 HSBC Securities USA\uc2 , questions the strength of 2H growth and bets the yield will end the year down around 2.6%. Ten-year notes up 20/32, yielding 2.818%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to hold daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the end of 2013 will help end the "feeding frenzy" surrounding the real, says Gradual Investimentos. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "wants to give companies that are being punished by the US dollar's appreciation a way out," firm says. Gradual notes dollar-denominated obligations held by companies are at $180B, up from $100B during the 2008 financial crisis. The goal is not to strengthen the real specifically to BRL2.20 per dollar, but to end speculation that it could weaken to BRL2.75, Gradual adds. BRL holding gains, with the dollar down 2.3% to BRL2.379. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Argentine bonds are mostly higher Friday despite a major ruling against the country in favor of holdout creditors. A US federal appeals court ruled that Argentina can't pay bonds it issued under a swap deal unless it pays the holdouts full value at the same time, but left a suspension of enforcement in place while the Supreme Court considers an appeal. The NY law, dollar-denominated 2017 bond is up 0.1% at ARS725. The NY law dollar-GDP warrants rose 0.1% to ARS71.00. (shane.romig@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:52 EDT - Plunge in July new-home sales, "combined with downward revision to earlier data, stand in stark contrast to the recent strong gains in the homebuilder survey," writes Joshua Shapiro, chief US economist at MFR Inc. Maybe July's an outlier, he says, but it's "certainly a warning signal" that conditions "are nowhere near as rosy as homebuilders, real estate agents, and their enablers in the media are so keen to represent." Not to mention, MBA data show weaker mortgage applications for purchases in recent months, suggesting that higher interest rates are having an impact on demand, "and also highlighting the fact that speculative demand continues to be the main driver of the existing home market." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:48 EDT - Gold prices are sharply higher as the annual rate of US new-home sales posted its steepest drop in 3 years last month. While the FOMC minutes on Wednesday offered no new clues as to the Fed's likely action on stimulus in September, traders remember his mantra about looking at economic data for guidance. With new-home sales plunging, some gold bugs are clearly hoping the Fed will stay the course at next month's meeting and keep supportive measures in place a while longer. Comex December gold is up 1.7% at $1,394.10/troy ounce. (tatyana.shumsky@wsj.com @MissShumsky)\par \par \uc2 Even in today's rebounding housing market, there's not a ton of new homes being sold each month, so it would stand to reason it's not that hard to get good reads--even initially--on activity. That's what makes the size of June's revision, cutting the annualized sales rate for the month 8.5% from initial data a month ago, so shocking. On an unadjusted basis, the Commerce Department said in July that 48K new homes were sold in June. Today, that number has been cut to 43K, with the biggest change coming in the South's numbers. And May's total has been dropped yet again, from 45K initially to 43K last month to 41K in today's report. July's initial number is 35K; what's the chance that doesn't get revised notably? (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrections & Amplifications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This item was corrected at 12:55 p.m. EDT because the original version incorrectly contained pricing data for April 2014 and 2015 instead of January of those years. Yields are now seen being 9.18% in January, down 6 basis points from Thursday's close, while the expected rate in January 2015 fell 23bp to 10.33%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 23, 2013 12:42 ET (16:42 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130823e98n004qa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
