\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Move to Calm Market Short Term\b0\par\par\uc2 1,386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2013\par 14:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:25 EDT - \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $60B \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  scheme unveiled late yesterday should calm the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market short term, but its efficacy could be limited further out barring deeper changes in economic policy, Espirito Santo Investment Bank says. Namely, a reining-in of domestic demand, without which the current-account balance could further deteriorate. "But the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were not being driven just by economic fundamentals, and it was bound to provoke an economic disruption if the government did not act," bank says. Real opens sharply stronger, recently trading at BRL2.3897 to the dollar. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:19 EDT - Down 13% the past 2 1/2 weeks, much of it following this week's downbeat F2Q report, Dick's (DKS) is liable to stay in the doghouse "until earnings growth re-accelerates and again tops expectations," says Oppenheimer as it downgrades the retailer to perform and cuts its price target to $44 from $56. A rebound could take a while as the investment bank anticipates "a potentially waning product cycle in categories such as golf, increasing competition and merchandising challenges in fitness to constrain topline growth." As such, "we remain concerned that slower trends lately in the sporting-goods sector could lead to more-aggressive promotions in coming months as retailers attempt to drive stronger traffic." DKS closed Thursday at $46.49. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:14 EDT - Raymond James cuts Pandora (P) to market perform from outperform, citing mainly valuation after shares have run up more than 135% YTD. P's 2Q results were strong, RJ says, "driven by increasing mobile monetization (mobile revs up 92% y/y) and lower content costs," though 3Q EPS guidance is below the firm's and Street's views due to increased investments. RJ also says listener metrics slowed in 2Q, "and the launch of Apple's iRadio (expected in September), could create some headline risk for shares." P down 6.6% premarket at $20.28. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:54 EDT - As constrained overhead costs helps ANN post a strong F2Q EPS beat, same-store-sales growth was underwhelming versus consensus views. But merchandise margins rose from a year ago, showing the Ann Taylor and Loft operator hasn't resorted to major discount in efforts to stoke demand. Whether ANN will be able to continue that is at question as Loft inventories jumped 18% while they rose 11% at the chains' outlets. With such locales posting SSS declines last quarter and Loft's regular stores logging 3.7% growth, supplies built up during the summer and will need to move. And while posting a muted 3Q sales view and again trimming the year's target, investors are liable to overlook the EPS beat today. ANN, inactive premarket, is up 2.9% this week. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:46 EDT - The $60B \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program unveiled by the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  late yesterday "seems good with the clarity and predictability welcomed in contrast to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and in scale is not a giant leap," says a London-based trader. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will offer some $19B in dollar credit lines, and the remainder in dollar swap contracts through year-end. Crucially, it "doesn't look like government [is] trying to impact the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too hard." (matthew.cowley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:43 EDT - \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2 's (QCOM) originals stem back to \uc2 Omnitracs\uc2 , a satellite-based location and messaging service geared toward truckers. Based on that CDMA-based system, the company moved into other CDMA technology that made the chip maker a cellphone leader. Now, QCOM is selling \uc2 Omnitracs\uc2  to Vista Equity for $800M, not even 1% of QCOM's $115B market cap. The entity long ago stopped being a core business of QCOM, but today's announcement is still a guidepost in a tech company's ability to transition and essentially come to lead in a new market, as QCOM has with cellphone chips. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EDT - US stock futures aren't carrying any momentum over from yesterday's rebound, early signs show a flat to slightly lower open. That's been one of the bulls' problems lately, an inability to generate or hold on to gains, an aspect which can confound those who've been attempting to buy the dip. Nasdaq's trading problems yesterday didn't have much impact on market sentiment, though investors will be exposed to a media post-mortem in the next couple days. Mixed session in Europe, Tokyo jumped 2.2% as USD strengthened vs JPY. S&P futures down a point; 10-yr note flat, yield at 2.90%. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:15 EDT - Down 20% from the spring's high, BMO says investors can get a best-in-class company at a discount as it upgrades \uc2 Simon Property\uc2  (SPG) to outperform. Besides a better entry point, the investment bank notes the shopping-center operator "has the largest exposure among peers to the attractive outlet business...and we believe outlet-store openings figure prominently in many retailers' growth plans." Meanwhile, BMO drops General Growth (GGP) to market perform as its prior valuation gap to SPG "has essentially been eliminated." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:07 EDT - Add \uc2 Foot Locker\uc2  (FL) to the retail crowd reporting weaker-than-expected same-store sales as it felt unexpected pressure in the US. But while F2Q SSS rose just 1.8%, CEO \uc2 Ken Hicks\uc2  says the company is still confident it will post a mid-single-digit increase for the year and see EPS rise at a double-digit rate. Always a bit risky to back-end hopes--especially as the long-elusive 2H economic rebound may well miss the US again this year. But inventories appear in check, meaning markdown risks should be limited for the time being. FL, down 5.9% this month, is inactive premarket. Shares have gone nowhere the past year. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 6:53 EDT - While Hibbett's (HIBB) F2Q revenue and EPS were in-line with consensus, same-store sales surprisingly barely rose as the sporting-goods retailer shows struggles that larger peer Dick's (DKS) reported earlier this week weren't entirely company-specific. As such, HIBB cuts its profit view for the year though "we are encouraged by a strong start to the third quarter." Shares, inactive premarket, have gone sideways for nearly 18 months. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 6:44 EDT - Despite a solid-looking F2Q, Gap (GPS) bear Sterne Agee says there's reason to be worried about the rest of the year--nevermind the "challenging retail environment" that the industry, not just apparel, has been facing. The investment bank notes GPS' merchandise margins fell last quarter, and with inventories up 10%/square foot they "put 2H margin profile at risk." But Janney, calling GPS' report "one of the best" so far, highlights inventory dollars/square foot rising a more-muted 6%, just above same-store-sales growth. The bank, which has been bullish, thinks GPS "will benefit from ongoing inventory discipline." Shares finished after-hours trading little changed. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The $60B \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program announced late Thursday by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  represents one of the boldest attempts yet by an emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the rout of recent weeks, says Capital Economics. "Under the new program, Brazil will sell as much \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in one month as \uc2 India's Reserve Bank\uc2  has sold in the past four." The researcher also noted a price-trend reversal isn't likely as "history suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment rather than prevent the adjustment from happening altogether. If sentiment continues to turn against EMs over the coming weeks and months, it seems likely that the real will fall further." (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 23, 2013 08:25 ET (12:25 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130823e98n002ts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
