\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Traders Short NYSE-Listed ETFs Amid Nasdaq Outage\b0\par\par\uc2 1,411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2013\par 20:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:13 EDT - Traders are shorting NYSE-listed ETFs in anticipation of rocky trading when the Nasdaq-listed stocks impacted by today's trading halt reopen, according to Dave Lutz, head of ETF trading strategy \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2 . Products like \uc2 iShares Russell 2000 ETF\uc2  (IWM) are seeing increased short-selling, he says, as investors look to hedge their positions. "This is unprecedented. There's uncertainly about what's going to happen when this is unlocked." (christopher.dieterich@wsj.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 14:03 EDT - Among economists who closely watch Canada, Capital Economics' \uc2 David Madani\uc2  has been a staunch bear and a lone voice in calling for \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to cut its policy rate. However, Madani today notes in a report that favorable oil prices could spur business investment in Canada, thus potentially lifting his 2013 and 2014 Canada GDP growth forecasts. Western Canada Select and Edmonton Par oil-price benchmarks are up 33% and 37%, respectively, he says, and with more of Canada's oil hitting US refineries through rail, that could be a boon for Canadian oil producers. Provided global oil prices remain buoyant, higher corporate earnings would presumably spur business investment growth and stronger economic growth, Madani said. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com, @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 Chances that Brazil will raise gasoline, diesel prices are rising, despite persistent inflationary pressures that have made the government reluctant to approve increases, says Banco Itau Chief Economist Ilan Goldfajn. Goldfajn notes the big differential between domestic and international fuel prices, which is hurting state-run oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (PBR, PETR4.BR). BRL's recent losses against USD of about 8% this week is also causing fuel-price spread to widen. But with inflation at 6.15% through mid-August, the government in a "difficult" spot, Goldfajn says. "It's quite likely that there will be a [fuel] price increase, but it won't be larger than single digits." (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The pound is off a bit today, but \uc2 Barclays\uc2  reckons it may be ripe for a more significant drop against the dollar. A key risk for sterling comes next week, when BOE Governor Carney will give a speech and may take the opportunity to express his concern with the back-up in UK rates--and the pound--since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unveiled new forward guidance that was intended to be dovish. "We would hedge for the risk that he uses the occasion to talk down rates more aggressively," note Aroop Chatterjee and Chris Walker at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . They recommend taking advantage of the drop in options costs to buy pound puts. GBP/USD is at 1.5586 from 1.5658, according to EBS via CQG. (matthew.walter@wsj.com; @mbwalter4)\par \par \uc2 The recent depreciation of the Brazilian real has left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slightly undervalued based on the long-term outlook for the country's economy, according to a \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  report. The bank's exchange-rate model puts the equilibrium rate at BRL2.34 per dollar. The real last traded at BRL2.43 to the dollar. Aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate hikes may support the real, but uncertainty about US interest rates and deteriorating trade and financial balances could further weaken it, according to the report. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 It wasn't a complete lack of buyers, but the $16B of 5-year TIPS just sold by the US drew just a 2.18 bid-to-cover, versus a 2.54 average. The lackluster demand comes even though the TIPS offered a -0.127% yield, the highest auction rate since April 2010. But investors are finding little reason to pay to own inflation protection these days when pricing pressure in the economy remains weak and the Fed is mulling reducing its inflation-inducing stimulus. Together, direct and indirect bidders took 46% of the offering, leaving primary dealers with more than half the sale. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real has come under intense pressure in recent days, retreating 6% so far in August and 16% so far in 2013, but most of the pressure is coming from the futures market, not spot trading, says Ilan Goldfajn, chief economist at Itau. "We're not seeing major outflows of dollars," says Goldfajn. "What we're seeing is a lot of action on the futures market. Everybody wants to be hedged." The pressure on the futures market has led \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  there, rather than in the spot market, using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts to help contain the real's decline. "Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market [by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] would be merely symbolic," says Goldfajn, himself a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:51 EDT - Nasdaq's (NDAQ) website says about 3,200 companies list stocks on its electronic exchange so with thousands now halted midday, it seems a bit of logistical nightmare to get them all back up and trading smoothly. Not that it can't be done, but it's probably not going to happen quickly, after the exchange halted trading in Nasdaq-listed securities about 30 minutes ago, citing technical issues. Fortunately, it comes at a time when trading volume isn't especially heavy, with all US exchanges' daily share volumes running close to the lowest levels of the year during this seasonally slow period. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue raising its Selic base rate, but the effort will merely halt the advance of inflation, not reverse it to any significant degree, says private Brazilian bank Itau in a report. Brazil's base rate is already a towering 8.5%, with Itau forecasting further rate hikes to 9.75% by the end of this year. But that will only be enough to hold inflation at its current level of about 6.1%. Nor will it have much effect on prices next year, with Itau predicting 2014 inflation of 6.0%. Problems range from price indexing to government spending, but the newest element is the depreciation of the Brazilian real, which will also contribute to inflation. The real has lost 16% against the dollar so far in 2013. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:29 EDT - Seems to be a major market disruption underway, with a headline from Nasdaq (NDAQ) at 12:19 pm saying all Nasdaq-listed securities were halted due to a tech issue. Dual-listed stocks such as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) and (CSCO) were still trading. Impact on overall market seems small so far, DJIA up 48, S&P 500 up 11. Nasdaq Comp was up 31.38, looks frozen there for now. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Brazil's economic recovery is losing steam as business confidence declines and inflation eats away at consumer purchasing power, according to a report by private Brazilian banking giant Itau. The bank estimates Brazil's economy expanded a 1.1% in the second quarter against the first quarter of 2013, but that's where the recovery ends. Itau is forecasting zero quarter-on-quarter growth in 3Q with the economy growing only 2.1% for the 2013 calendar year as a whole. Persistent inflation and the higher interest rates needed to fight it will mean even lower growth, seen at just 1.7% in 2014, according to the bank. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:14 EDT - The PHLX Semiconductor Index, or SOX, is teetering just above a key support line, the break of which could threaten the longer-term uptrend. SOX closed yesterday below an uptrend line starting at the November low, before bouncing back above it today. SOX up 1% at 463, while the uptrend line extends today to 460-461. Breaking the trendline could target a test of what \uc2 Ladenburg Thalmann\uc2  technician Adolfo Rueda calls the "intermediate-term trend" at the 40-week moving average around 432. SOX needs to "reclaim its short-term trend," Rueda says, by rising above resistance at 470-475, to turn the short-term outlook back to bullish. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-22-13 1413ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020130822e98m000d1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
