\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: NY Fed Survey Shows $15B QE Cut Expected in September\b0\par\par\uc2 1,406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2013\par 20:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NY Fed just released results from a survey of primary dealers taken before the July FOMC meeting. Median expectation was for the Fed to start reducing bond purchases in September, cutting $45B Treasurys purchases and $40B MBS purchases each month to $35B apiece. That pace is expected to hold at the October Fed meeting, then go down to $30B and $25B, respectively, at the December meeting. The survey noted that "several" of the dealers said these expectations were partly formed by looking at what Fed meetings had press conferences -- a setup that's likely seen as more conducive for the Fed to explain its actions. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Chances that Brazil will raise gasoline, diesel prices are rising, despite persistent inflationary pressures that have made the government reluctant to approve increases, says Banco Itau Chief Economist Ilan Goldfajn. Goldfajn notes the big differential between domestic and international fuel prices, which is hurting state-run oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (PBR, PETR4.BR). BRL's recent losses against USD of about 8% this week is also causing fuel-price spread to widen. But with inflation at 6.15% through mid-August, the government in a "difficult" spot, Goldfajn says. "It's quite likely that there will be a [fuel] price increase, but it won't be larger than single digits." (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The pound is off a bit today, but \uc2 Barclays\uc2  reckons it may be ripe for a more significant drop against the dollar. A key risk for sterling comes next week, when BOE Governor Carney will give a speech and may take the opportunity to express his concern with the back-up in UK rates--and the pound--since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unveiled new forward guidance that was intended to be dovish. "We would hedge for the risk that he uses the occasion to talk down rates more aggressively," note Aroop Chatterjee and Chris Walker at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . They recommend taking advantage of the drop in options costs to buy pound puts. GBP/USD is at 1.5586 from 1.5658, according to EBS via CQG. (matthew.walter@wsj.com; @mbwalter4)\par \par \uc2 The recent depreciation of the Brazilian real has left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slightly undervalued based on the long-term outlook for the country's economy, according to a \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  report. The bank's exchange-rate model puts the equilibrium rate at BRL2.34 per dollar. The real last traded at BRL2.43 to the dollar. Aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate hikes may support the real, but uncertainty about US interest rates and deteriorating trade and financial balances could further weaken it, according to the report. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 It wasn't a complete lack of buyers, but the $16B of 5-year TIPS just sold by the US drew just a 2.18 bid-to-cover, versus a 2.54 average. The lackluster demand comes even though the TIPS offered a -0.127% yield, the highest auction rate since April 2010. But investors are finding little reason to pay to own inflation protection these days when pricing pressure in the economy remains weak and the Fed is mulling reducing its inflation-inducing stimulus. Together, direct and indirect bidders took 46% of the offering, leaving primary dealers with more than half the sale. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real has come under intense pressure in recent days, retreating 6% so far in August and 16% so far in 2013, but most of the pressure is coming from the futures market, not spot trading, says Ilan Goldfajn, chief economist at Itau. "We're not seeing major outflows of dollars," says Goldfajn. "What we're seeing is a lot of action on the futures market. Everybody wants to be hedged." The pressure on the futures market has led \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  there, rather than in the spot market, using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts to help contain the real's decline. "Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market [by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] would be merely symbolic," says Goldfajn, himself a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue raising its Selic base rate, but the effort will merely halt the advance of inflation, not reverse it to any significant degree, says private Brazilian bank Itau in a report. Brazil's base rate is already a towering 8.5%, with Itau forecasting further rate hikes to 9.75% by the end of this year. But that will only be enough to hold inflation at its current level of about 6.1%. Nor will it have much effect on prices next year, with Itau predicting 2014 inflation of 6.0%. Problems range from price indexing to government spending, but the newest element is the depreciation of the Brazilian real, which will also contribute to inflation. The real has lost 16% against the dollar so far in 2013. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Brazil's economic recovery is losing steam as business confidence declines and inflation eats away at consumer purchasing power, according to a report by private Brazilian banking giant Itau. The bank estimates Brazil's economy expanded a 1.1% in the second quarter against the first quarter of 2013, but that's where the recovery ends. Itau is forecasting zero quarter-on-quarter growth in 3Q with the economy growing only 2.1% for the 2013 calendar year as a whole. Persistent inflation and the higher interest rates needed to fight it will mean even lower growth, seen at just 1.7% in 2014, according to the bank. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1558 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] FTSE 100 ends 0.9% higher at 6446.87, in line with broader European gains. "After the Fed-induced declines of recent days, it's nice to see markets concentrating on the more mundane drivers of economic data and reacting to a broad macro improvement in manufacturing data from both China and Germany," says Michael Hewson at CMC Markets. The upbeat Chinese data lift mining stocks, with \uc2 Glencore\uc2  Xstrata +3% and \uc2 Vedanta Resources\uc2  +2.9%. IMI is the standout gainer, rising 5.8% after 1H results. Wolseley follows close behind, up 4.3% after a broker upgrade. Friday, the second release of 2Q UK GDP is at 0830 GMT with US new home sales at 1400 GMT. (michele.maatouk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1557 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] FTSE 100 ends 0.9% higher at 6446.87, in line with broader European gains. "After the Fed-induced declines of recent days, it's nice to see markets concentrating on the more mundane drivers of economic data and reacting to a broad macro improvement in manufacturing data from both China and Germany," says Michael Hewson at CMC Markets. The upbeat Chinese data lift mining stocks, with \uc2 Glencore\uc2  Xstrata +3% and \uc2 Vedanta Resources\uc2  +2.9%. IMI is the standout gainer, rising 5.8% after 1H results. Wolseley follows close behind, up 4.3% after a broker upgrade. Friday, the second release of 2Q UK GDP is at 0830 GMT with US new home sales at 1400 GMT. (michele.maatouk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso might have found a floor after falling seven straight sessions and closing Wednesday at a 6-week low of COP1,922 per dollar. The decline in COP has largely been driven by fears that Fed tapering could start as early as next month, but those worries seem to be subsiding for now in Bogota, where COP is treading water at 1,922.25/USD, according to Set-\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 A decline in Brazil's unemployment rate could give beleaguered President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  a lift, says Jankiel Santos of Espirito Santo Investment Bank. Unemployment fell to 5.6% in July versus 6.0% in June. Despite gloomy outlook for Brazil's economic prospects, "the current situation seems to be good enough for entrepreneurs to hire new employees," Santos says. Economist also says that a recent decline in monthly wages won't affect consumption, as pay raises should continue to be "generous." "This is good news for retailers, but bad news for inflation in the services group," where raises get passed along to consumers, Santos says. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com) [ 22-08-13 1845GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130822e98m000nm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
