\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Brazil Real Mainly Pressured from Futures Market -Itau\b0\par\par\uc2 1,339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2013\par 18:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real has come under intense pressure in recent days, retreating 6% so far in August and 16% so far in 2013, but most of the pressure is coming from the futures market, not spot trading, says Ilan Goldfajn, chief economist at Itau. "We're not seeing major outflows of dollars," says Goldfajn. "What we're seeing is a lot of action on the futures market. Everybody wants to be hedged." The pressure on the futures market has led \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  there, rather than in the spot market, using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts to help contain the real's decline. "Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market [by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] would be merely symbolic," says Goldfajn, himself a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:51 EDT - Nasdaq's (NDAQ) website says about 3,200 companies list stocks on its electronic exchange so with thousands now halted midday, it seems a bit of logistical nightmare to get them all back up and trading smoothly. Not that it can't be done, but it's probably not going to happen quickly, after the exchange halted trading in Nasdaq-listed securities about 30 minutes ago, citing technical issues. Fortunately, it comes at a time when trading volume isn't especially heavy, with all US exchanges' daily share volumes running close to the lowest levels of the year during this seasonally slow period. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue raising its Selic base rate, but the effort will merely halt the advance of inflation, not reverse it to any significant degree, says private Brazilian bank Itau in a report. Brazil's base rate is already a towering 8.5%, with Itau forecasting further rate hikes to 9.75% by the end of this year. But that will only be enough to hold inflation at its current level of about 6.1%. Nor will it have much effect on prices next year, with Itau predicting 2014 inflation of 6.0%. Problems range from price indexing to government spending, but the newest element is the depreciation of the Brazilian real, which will also contribute to inflation. The real has lost 16% against the dollar so far in 2013. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:29 EDT - Seems to be a major market disruption underway, with a headline from Nasdaq (NDAQ) at 12:19 pm saying all Nasdaq-listed securities were halted due to a tech issue. Dual-listed stocks such as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) and (CSCO) were still trading. Impact on overall market seems small so far, DJIA up 48, S&P 500 up 11. Nasdaq Comp was up 31.38, looks frozen there for now. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Brazil's economic recovery is losing steam as business confidence declines and inflation eats away at consumer purchasing power, according to a report by private Brazilian banking giant Itau. The bank estimates Brazil's economy expanded a 1.1% in the second quarter against the first quarter of 2013, but that's where the recovery ends. Itau is forecasting zero quarter-on-quarter growth in 3Q with the economy growing only 2.1% for the 2013 calendar year as a whole. Persistent inflation and the higher interest rates needed to fight it will mean even lower growth, seen at just 1.7% in 2014, according to the bank. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:14 EDT - The PHLX Semiconductor Index, or SOX, is teetering just above a key support line, the break of which could threaten the longer-term uptrend. SOX closed yesterday below an uptrend line starting at the November low, before bouncing back above it today. SOX up 1% at 463, while the uptrend line extends today to 460-461. Breaking the trendline could target a test of what \uc2 Ladenburg Thalmann\uc2  technician Adolfo Rueda calls the "intermediate-term trend" at the 40-week moving average around 432. SOX needs to "reclaim its short-term trend," Rueda says, by rising above resistance at 470-475, to turn the short-term outlook back to bullish. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso might have found a floor after falling seven straight sessions and closing Wednesday at a 6-week low of COP1,922 per dollar. The decline in COP has largely been driven by fears that Fed tapering could start as early as next month, but those worries seem to be subsiding for now in Bogota, where COP is treading water at 1,922.25/USD, according to Set-\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:34 EDT - US consumer demand for pork is "pretty solid" despite higher prices for some products, including bacon, Hormel (HRL) CEO Jeffrey Ettinger tells Dow Jones. HRL and other pork processors have recently raised bacon prices after the cost of pork bellies climbed, but "bacon demand has actually hung in there pretty well." Ettinger notes higher retail beef prices this year have steered some consumers to pork, which costs less per pound. (david.kesmodel@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:29 EDT - \uc2 GameStop\uc2  (GME) predictably pops on its F2Q earnings beat and rosy outlook for the rest of the year on perceived strength of new game consoles from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) and \uc2 Sony\uc2  (SNE) due in November. But is there more to it? Consumer weakness has pushed retailers in general to pull back on 2H views, so why would shoppers eschew all else just to save for high-priced new consoles, games and accessories? Also worth noting the nearly 22% short interest in GME shares. They're up 11% at $53 after rising as much as 18% earlier to hit a fresh 5-year high. (michael.calia@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 A decline in Brazil's unemployment rate could give beleaguered President Dilma Rousseff a lift, says Jankiel Santos of Espirito Santo Investment Bank. Unemployment fell to 5.6% in July versus 6.0% in June. Despite gloomy outlook for Brazil's economic prospects, "the current situation seems to be good enough for entrepreneurs to hire new employees," Santos says. Economist also says that a recent decline in monthly wages won't affect consumption, as pay raises should continue to be "generous." "This is good news for retailers, but bad news for inflation in the services group," where raises get passed along to consumers, Santos says. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:16 EDT - Spam maker Hormel (HRL) expects to benefit from lower feed costs amid forecasts for big US corn and soybean harvests this fall, but it's too early to gauge how much, CEO Jeffrey Ettinger tells Dow Jones. "We have sort of a guarded outlook toward what the crop will ultimately be. We have been through years where things can look good and then" adverse weather \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Corn futures are about 40% below record highs set last August. (david.kesmodel@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 After signaling at the end of July about a possible reduction in shorter-dated note sales given shrinking fiscal deficits and improving tax receipts, Treasury says next week's 2-year sale will be $1B less than what's been the case at $34B. Supply for 5s and 7s will remain at $35B and $29B, respectively. A sale of 3-year notes in September is likely the next one to be cut by $1B, some traders say. Lower supply may be a cushion for rising yields amid taper fears, but given the reduction is for shorter-dated notes, it won't benefit longer-dated bonds--which have bore the brunt of the selloff. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 22, 2013 12:56 ET (16:56 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130822e98m008oh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
