\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real sinks to near 5-year low after Fed minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2013\par 17:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates throughout) MEXICO CITY, Aug 21 (Reuters) - Brazil's real plunged to a near five-year low on Wednesday, leading losses across Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors looked at the minutes of the U.S. Federal Reserve's latest policy meeting for clues as to the timing of a stimulus wind-down. The Mexican peso and the Brazilian real have tumbled for at least six of the past seven sessions on fears that the Fed is about to cut back on its bond-buying program, which for years has provided a steady source of dollars seeking higher returns in emerging markets. Minutes from the Fed's July meeting, released on Wednesday, showed members of the Federal Open Market Committee had different opinions as to when the Fed should start winding down its bond purchases. The overall view, however, was that the statement did not materially change the market's expectation of a September tapering.\par \par \uc2  The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plummeted 2.3 percent to close at 2.4501 per greenback, its weakest level since December 2008. The real's tumble prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce it would offer to sell up to $4 billion on the spot market through repurchase agreements on Thursday, the second time this week that the bank has resorted to this form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling swaps in the futures market to provide investors with a hedge against a further depreciation of the real, which has already lost more than 18 percent of its value since the beginning of May. Mexico's peso slid 2.23 percent to 13.27 per dollar, its lowest level and biggest drop since late June. Also weighing on the Mexican peso was local data showing retail sales were stagnant in June, one day after the government lowered its 2013 growth estimate to 1.8 percent from 3.1 percent on disappointing growth data. The Fed's program currently injects $85 billion a month into the market, and investors and economists forecast it could be reduced by something between $10 billion and $20 billion as of next month.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices at 2156 GMT:\par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                        Daily  YTD pct\par                                     pct   change\par                          Latest  change  \par  Brazil real             2.4501    -2.3   -16.79\par                                          \par  Mexico peso              13.27   -2.23    -3.06\par                                          \par  Chile peso              514.90   -0.14    -7.03\par                                          \par  Colombia peso          1926.00   -0.21    -8.31\par                                          \par  Peru sol                2.8130    0.04    -9.31\par                                          \par  Argentina peso          5.6025   -0.09   -12.32\par  (interbank)                             \par                                          \par  Argentina peso            9.00   -0.33   -24.67\par  (parallel)                              \par                                          \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Writing by Alexandra Alper and Walter Brandimarte; editing by G Crosse)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | M | RBN | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130824e98l0006y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
