\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \uc2 Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  to Buy Back Dollars at BRL2.5179, BRL2.4674\b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2013\par 18:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Buy Back Dollars at BRL2.5179, BRL2.4674," at 11:11 a.m. EDT, misstated in the fourth paragraph that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars for 2.399 reais. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars for 2.399 reais.\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second and third time in the country's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday to rollover expiring swap contracts and to sell dollar credit lines.\par \par \uc2 The bank sold all of the 20,000 swap contracts that it will use to replace existing contracts that expire at the start of next month. The bank sold $986.3 million in the swap auction.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held a swap auction at which it sold $993.4 million in new swap contracts, placing all 20,000 contracts it offered. The swap contracts allow investors with bonds linked to local interest rates to exchange them for paper indexed to the U.S. dollar. The auctions typically help the real appreciate against the U.S. dollar by offering investors increased options for hedging the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also offered dollar credit lines at a Tuesday auction. The bank sold dollars to the market, with the promise to buy back the dollars in January and April of next year. The bank bought dollars for 2.399 reais and committed to repurchasing those dollars on Jan. 2 for a price of BRL2.4674 per dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday. On April 1, it will buy back dollars at BRL2.5179.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been picking up the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days as it tries to slow the slide in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real remains close to its weakest level since March 2009. The real was trading at BRL2.4007 per dollar after the credit line auction, after opening the day at BRL2.3862 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The expected unwinding of easy-money policies in the U.S., together with sluggish growth at home and deteriorating government accounts, have caused the real to plunge against the dollar, making it one of the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Winterstein at paulo.winterstein@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20-08-13 1639GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020130820e98k000he\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
