\par\par \b \uc2 Rupee Hits Record Low as Tapering Expectations Prompt Selloff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Davidson   \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2013\par 21:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee sank to a record low Monday, leading a broad-based selloff of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on expectations that the Federal Reserve will begin to wind down its fiscal stimulus as soon as September.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real hit a four-year low against the dollar, while the Turkish lira and Hungarian forint hit multiweek lows. Despite the absence of economic data from the U.S. on Monday, investors continued to gear up for the possibility of less Fed stimulus, which pushed \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields higher and drove down emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Stronger U.S. economic data in recent weeks has reinforced expectations that the Fed will start to taper its $85 billion-a-month bond-buying program in September. This asset-purchasing program has kept rates low in the U.S. and increased liquidity across global markets, making emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 "The market is yet again trying to gauge when Fed tapering will be happening," said Guillaume Salomon, emerging-market strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "All of this is happening on the background of [Group of Seven countries'] yields going higher and becoming a competitor for emerging-market yields, especially when central banks have been cutting rates over the past couple of years. This is all quite a challenging environment for [emerging markets]."\par \par \uc2 Countries such as India that have large current account deficits have been particularly affected by the emerging-market selloff, as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are seen as less resilient because these countries rely on foreign capital flows to fund their current-account gaps.\par \par \uc2 The dollar gained more than 2% against the rupee, to trade as high as INR63.58. It traded recently at INR63.33, according to CQG data.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market assets took an additional hit after the Bundesbank said Monday that its forward guidance isn't set in stone, which the markets read as a hint that rates could rise earlier than expected.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah slid to a four-year low Monday, falling 1.6% against the U.S. dollar, which bought IDR10,551.0. On Friday, data showed that the country's current account deficit widened to $9.8 billion in the second quarter from $5.8 billion previously, due to rising imports and declining exports.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Asia, the Thai baht fell to THB31.410 against the dollar after Thailand's economic activity contracted by a seasonally adjusted 0.3% in the second quarter, meaning the country is technically in a recession. Exports fell 1.4% on the previous quarter on the back of waning demand from China, Thailand's largest market. The country's current account balance also swung into the red, printing a $5.1 billion deficit compared with a $1.3 billion surplus in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Commodity-linked Latin American countries, which rely heavily on exports, also suffered as Fed tapering concerns dragged down commodity prices. The Brazilian real weakened to a four-year low as the dollar climbed above BRL2.40 for the first time since March 2009. Despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real has continued to fall amid \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's waning growth potential, doggedly high inflation and a large current account deficit. \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times Monday to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts aimed at stabilizing the real. The dollar bought BRL2.4145 late Monday, from BRL2.3927 the previous session, according to CQG.\par \par \uc2 The dollar gained some 1% against the Mexican peso, recently buying MXN13.0664. The greenback also climbed against the Chilean peso, changing hands at CLP518.80 compared with CLP512.70 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 The South African rand, which often acts as a proxy for other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it tends to move in line with broader market sentiment, fell almost 1% to the dollar, which bought ZAR10.1891.\par \par \uc2 In emerging Europe, the Hungarian forint fell to an eight-week low against the euro, which recently bought HUF300.40. The Turkish lira sank to one month-low against the dollar, which changed hands at TRL1.9535.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lauren Davidson at lauren.davidson@dowjones.com [ 19-08-13 1949GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130819e98j000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
