\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Strengthens As Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2 , U.S. Data Disappoint\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paulo Winterstein   \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2013\par 22:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened Monday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, and the real joined other global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in gaining against the dollar after disappointing economic data from the world's biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.2316 to the U.S. dollar, slightly stronger than Friday's close at BRL2.2399 per dollar, according to Tullet Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 The real weakened against the dollar in early trading as economists once more cut their estimates for growth this year and next. A weekly survey by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  published Monday showed analysts expect gross domestic product to expand 2.28% this year and 2.6% in 2014, down from the previous week's forecast of 2.31% growth in 2013 and 2.8% next year.\par \par \uc2 The government also cut its own growth estimates, now seeing the economy expanding 3% this year, down from the 3.5% previously. The government, seeing lower growth, said it would cut spending this year to ensure that it meets its primary surplus target. On Monday Finance Minister Guido Mantega said he would freeze BRL10 billion in planned spending this year, bringing the total amount of spending the government is freezing this year to BRL38 billion.\par \par \uc2 Further jolting markets was a third day of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Since Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been holding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap contract auctions to replace expiring contracts. Some $5.7 billion in such contracts are due to expire on Aug. 1, with another $5 billion expiring Sept. 2, and the bank has been acting daily to roll over those contracts and keep the market flush with dollars.\par \par \uc2 The swap contracts offer investors the opportunity to exchange bonds linked to domestic interest rates for paper indexed directly to the U.S. dollar, and they effectively support the real against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But what finally drove the real up against the dollar was disappointing data from the U.S., which showed existing-home sales fell 1.2% in June. Economists had predicted that sales would rise 1.9%, and the weak data drove down the dollar against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A weak U.S. economy means that the Federal Reserve is less likely to end its easy-money policies. That expansionist policy has helped drive investment to emerging markets in the past, strengthening those countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Winterstein at paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com [ 22-07-13 2027GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130722e97m000m6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
