\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real falls to 4-yr low; Mexico peso gains on Bernanke\b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2013\par 23:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, July 10 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real closed at its weakest level in more than four years on Wednesday as minutes from the Federal Reserve's latest monetary policy meeting failed to allay investor concerns about an eventual withdrawal of U.S. stimulus measures. The minutes showed that even as consensus built within the Fed about the likely need to begin pulling back on economic stimulus measures soon, officials wanted more reassurance that the employment recovery was on solid ground before a policy retreat. Analysts interpreted the meeting minutes as a sign that the Fed will be more cautious before pulling the plug on its stimulus program, which for years has supported appetite for higher-yielding, emerging market assets. The minutes helped Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  erase some of their losses, but did not stop the real or the Chilean peso from closing in the red. Investors only became more optimistic after Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  later said the U.S.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to pursue an accommodative monetary policy for now as a recent improvement in the employment rate may be overstating the health of the labor market. Bernanke's comments pushed the Mexican peso higher, but came too late for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil and Chile, which had already settled for the day. * Mexico's peso gained 0.5 percent after Bernanke's remarks. It was down around 0.5 percent before the release of the Fed's meeting minutes, but erased most of its losses afterward. * Brazil's real ended 0.5 percent lower at 2.2723 per dollar, its weakest level since the beginning of April 2009. Traders said foreign investors continued to sell Brazilian assets as other markets looked set to achieve a better performance in the short term. * The real slid even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market with an auction of traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, derivative contracts that provide investors with a hedge against a further depreciation of the real. * Chile's peso lost 0.3 percent as concerns about the future of U.S. stimulus eclipsed a rise in the price of copper, the country's main export product. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices at 2135 GMT:\par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             Daily  YTD pct\par                                          pct   change\par                              Latest   change  \par  Brazil real                 2.2723    -0.50   -10.22\par                                               \par  Mexico peso                12.8460     0.47     0.14\par                                               \par  Chile peso                 508.700    -0.26    -5.90\par                                               \par  Colombia peso            1,919.100     0.10    -7.98\par                                               \par  Peru sol                    2.7780    -0.11    -8.17\par                                               \par  Argentina peso              5.4125    -0.09    -9.24\par  Argentina peso              8.0400    -0.75   -15.67\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | M | RBN | NAT | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130713e97a0009e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
