\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  fall on Fed, China concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2013\par 21:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte RIO DE JANEIRO, June 20 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped sharply on Thursday as markets continued to reel from the U.S. Federal Reserve saying on Wednesday that it may scale back its stimulus measures. Economic data on Thursday showing Chinese factory activity fell to a nine-month low in June added to the sense of global unease as investors feared emerging market countries will also suffer from falling demand and prices for their commodities exports. The selling in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday extended Wednesday's losses as investors continued to digest the implications of the Fed's actions, which could reduce global liquidity. Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  said the bank's stimulus program, which for years has been providing a steady source of dollars seeking higher returns in emerging markets, will likely be reduced this year and finished by mid-2014.\par \par \uc2  * Brazil's real lost more than 2 percent to hit an intraday low of 2.275 per dollar, its weakest level in more than four years. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost nearly 12 percent since the beginning of May, complicating government efforts to rein in inflation. * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the market but was unable to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Policymakers initially sold traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, which offer protection against possible additional losses in the real. In the afternoon, they offered to sell greenbacks directly on the spot market with repurchase agreement, in a strategy to provide temporary dollar liquidity to the market. * Mexico's peso slid more than 1 percent to as much as 13.457, its weakest level since August 2012, leaving investors on watch for a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . * Chile's peso ended 2.8 percent lower at 513.90 per dollar, its weakest level since the beginning of June 2012. * Colombia's peso slumped 2.3 percent, its largest single-day loss since December 2010, increasing losses\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in the year-to-date to nearly 9 percent.   \par     Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices at 1910 GMT: \par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                      change   change\par                             Latest           \par  Brazil real                2.2616    -1.85    -9.80\par                                              \par  Mexico peso               13.3800    -1.27    -3.86\par                                              \par  Chile peso               513.9000    -2.80    -6.85\par                                              \par  Colombia peso           1939.7200    -2.25    -8.96\par                                              \par  Peru sol                   2.7900    -1.72    -8.57\par                                              \par  Argentina peso             5.3475    -0.09    -8.13\par  Argentina peso             8.5200     0.35   -20.42\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130623e96k0008z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
